# AirportIQ Core Architecture

## Source of Truth

Python and verified aviation data are the source of truth.

The LLM is an interface, orchestration, and explanation layer.

The LLM must never create or modify:

- airport statistics
- KPIs
- congestion values
- long-haul percentages
- unmet-demand indices
- opportunity scores
- confidence levels
- airport rankings

All numerical analytical results must originate from deterministic backend functions.

## Layer Boundaries

Maintain this flow:

User
→ React
→ FastAPI
→ Agent
→ Approved Tool
→ Service
→ Analytics / Scoring
→ Data Provider

Do not skip layers without a clear reason.

React must not implement business calculations.

FastAPI routes must not implement business logic.

Providers must not implement scoring.

The LLM must not directly call arbitrary external APIs.

## Simplicity

Prefer the simplest implementation that satisfies the requirement.

Do not introduce:

- microservices
- event buses
- message queues
- vector databases
- RAG
- Redis
- authentication
- unnecessary design patterns

unless explicitly requested.

## Files

Keep files focused on one responsibility.

Prefer small functions and clear naming.

Do not create unnecessary wrapper files.

## Codebase Navigation

Before making substantial multi-file changes, read:

docs/CODEBASE_MAP.md

Use it to identify the correct layer and canonical files.

Do not blindly search the entire repository if the map already identifies the responsible module.

If architecture, responsibilities, important files, or major execution flows change, update docs/CODEBASE_MAP.md.

Minor implementation changes do not require updating the map.

## Guardrails

Never weaken existing AI or data guardrails simply to make a feature work.

Never silently fall back from deterministic calculations to LLM-generated values.

When data is missing, preserve explicit uncertainty.

## Verification

After significant implementation work, use the verify-feature skill.

For AI behavior changes, also use test-agent.

For security-sensitive changes, use security-review.

For major architectural changes, use architecture-check.